<!--
    arm64e pointer-auth failure checks (`option ptrauthtrap on|off`).

    Issue: Apple's clang checks an authenticated pointer inline because a CPU
    without FEAT_FPAC does not trap on a failed aut*. Before a tail call it
    tests bits 62 and 61 of the authenticated x30 and traps with
    `brk #0xc471`. SLEIGH hides the autibsp, so the bit test reads the incoming
    return address and every such function of an arm64e iOS system daemon
    (205 of them) printed
    `if ((v1 ^ v1 << 1) >> 0x3e & 1) { (*SoftwareBreakpoint(0xc471,..))(); }`
    ahead of its tail call.

    Stage / sub-stage: P2 flow-classification. `p2_lift/kuna_ptrauthtrap.rs`
    anchors on the trap block (one brk whose SoftwareBreakpoint immediate is
    0xc470..0xc473) and removes pre-SSA each CBRANCH edge into it whose guard
    is the bit test of the return address (x30 reloaded from the frame or
    unwritten in the function) and that is taken when the bits disagree. The
    shape is the evidence; no image format is consulted, so the bytechunks
    below exercise it through the XML path too.

    P0 assertion: `option ptrauthtrap on|off`.

    The XML wrapper only supplies the harness architecture; every pass loads
    the `macho_arm64e_auth` fixture (an arm64e PAC00 image, see the fixtures
    README) through the object bootstrap.

    The bytechunk `x30check` is the real LLVM check on plain AArch64 code
    (`ldp x29, x30, [sp], #0x10; autibsp; eor x16, x30, x30, lsl #1;
    tbz x16, #62, 1f; brk #0xc471; 1: ret`): the check is cut. The bytechunk
    `x30forged` is the same bit test on a value the code moved into x30
    (`mov x30, x0`): that check stays. So do `x30forgedearlier` (the
    `mov x30, x1` sits in the block before the test), `x30loaded`
    (`ldr x30, [x0]`, not a frame reload) and `x30inverted` (the real
    reload and test, but `tbz` branches to the trap when the bits agree, so
    every authentic return address traps).

    The bytechunk `both` is a guard whose two arms are both traps
    (`tbz x0, #62, 1f; brk #0xc471; 1: brk #0xc472`). No arm survives to fall
    through to, so the fold leaves it whole. The bytechunk `plain` is a plain
    AArch64 compare guarding a pointer-auth immediate (`cmp w0, #0x2a; b.ne 1f;
    mov w0, #1; ret; 1: brk #0xc470`): the immediate alone is no evidence, so
    the check stays. The last pass loads `macho_arm64e_brk`, an arm64e image
    whose `_check` is the same compare and whose `_check_x0` is the bit test of
    x0 rather than x30 (`eor x16, x0, x0, lsl #1; tbz x16, #62; brk #0xc472`):
    both checks stay on arm64e too.

    Pass 1 (`option ptrauthtrap off`) reproduces the BUG: `_tail_auth` carries
    the bit test, the trap call and a variable for the incoming x30.
    Pass 2 (`option ptrauthtrap on`, the shipped default) is the FIX:
    `_tail_auth` is the call to `_helper` and the tail call to `_helper2`.
    `_strip_check`'s xpacd/cmp form folds without the option, so its body is
    the same plain load in both passes.
-->
